message.content:
{"passages":[{"reference":"1.5","text":"What, then, must be done except this? I will travel to Egypt, befriend the hierophants and prophets of the inner sanctuaries, and, after seeking and finding a magician, persuade him with a large sum of money to perform a sending up of a soul—the practice called necromancy—while I make an inquiry as though about some particular matter. But the inquiry will be about learning whether the soul is immortal. I will know the soul’s answer—that it is immortal—not because it has spoken or I have even heard it, but merely because it has appeared, so that, having seen it with my own eyes, I may have a self-sufficient and adequate conclusion from its mere appearance that it exists. The obscure words that belong to hearing will then no longer be able to overturn the eyes’ own evidence.

Nevertheless, I put this very plan before a philosopher of my acquaintance, who advised me in many ways not to dare it. “For if the soul does not heed the magician,” he said, “you will have acted against the laws that forbid people to do such things, and you will live with a guilty conscience. But if it does heed him, then, besides your living with a guilty conscience, I think you will no longer make progress in piety, for whose sake you even dared this. For they say that the divine is hostile toward those who harass souls at the dissolution of dead bodies.” When I heard this, I became more hesitant to undertake such a thing, but I did not abandon my original intention. Instead, I grew despondent, as though my impulse had been obstructed.","lexical_decisions":[{"source_quote":"ψυχῆς ἀναπομπὴν (τὴν λεγομένην νεκρομαντείαν)","chosen":"a sending up of a soul—the practice called necromancy","rationale":"“Sending up” preserves the directional image of ἀναπομπή rather than replacing it with the broader “summoning”; the parenthetical identification is retained."},{"source_quote":"οὐκ ἐκ τοῦ λαλῆσαι ἢ καὶ ἀκοῦσαι","chosen":"not because it has spoken or I have even heard it","rationale":"The infinitives do not state their subjects. Context supports the soul as speaker and Clement as hearer; the more impersonal “not from speaking or even hearing” is also grammatically possible."},{"source_quote":"αὐτάρκη καὶ ἱκανὴν ἀπόφασιν","chosen":"a self-sufficient and adequate conclusion","rationale":"Here ἀπόφασις denotes a determination or conclusion, not the unrelated sense “denial.”"},{"source_quote":"τὰ τῶν ὀφθαλμῶν ἴδια τὰ τῆς ἀκοῆς ἀνατρέψαι ἄδηλα ῥήματα","chosen":"The obscure words that belong to hearing will then no longer be able to overturn the eyes’ own evidence","rationale":"The obscure words are the grammatical agent, and what belongs to the eyes is the object. “Evidence” makes the substantival τὰ … ἴδια intelligible without changing the contrast between sight and hearing."},{"source_quote":"δυσσυνειδήτως βιώσεις","chosen":"you will live with a guilty conscience","rationale":"The adverb denotes living with a bad or troubled conscience, not merely living unhappily."},{"source_quote":"τοῖς τῇ λύσει θανόντων σωμάτων σκύλλουσιν τὰς ψυχάς","chosen":"those who harass souls at the dissolution of dead bodies","rationale":"The dative τῇ λύσει is read temporally or circumstantially, without making the harassers the explicit agents of the dissolution. “Through the dissolution of dead bodies” is a viable instrumental alternative. Reading λύσις as the release of souls from bodies is possible in context, but the supplied construction does not itself explicitly say that the necromancers release them."}],"source_issues":[]},{"reference":"1.6","text":"And, so that I may not recount such matters to you at great length, while I was occupied with so many reflections and circumstances, a certain report arose quietly during the reign of Tiberius Caesar. Beginning at the spring equinox, it continually grew and, truly a good messenger of God, ran throughout the world, unable to keep silent and conceal God’s will. It therefore became more widespread and greater each time, saying that a certain man in Judea, having begun at the spring equinox, was announcing to the Jews the good news of the kingdom of the everlasting God. He said that any one of them would enjoy it if he first set his way of life right. And to secure belief in him—that he did these things while full of divinity—he performed many marvelous signs and wonders by command alone, as one who had received authority from God. For he made the deaf hear and the blind see again; he made those with deformed limbs walk and set the lame upright; he drove away every disease and put every demon to flight. Even people with leprosy or scabies were healed and went away merely by fixing their gaze on him from a distance. Dead people brought to him were raised, and there was nothing he was unable to do. Indeed, the further time advanced, the far greater and more firmly established became—not the report any longer, I mean, but the truth of the matter—because of the increasing number of visitors. Assemblies were already forming in various places for deliberation and inquiry into who the one who had appeared might be and what he meant to say.","lexical_decisions":[{"source_quote":"ἐξ ἐαρινῆς τροπῆς","chosen":"at the spring equinox","rationale":"τροπή can denote a seasonal turning point, including an equinox. “At the turn of spring” is a less technically specific alternative."},{"source_quote":"ὡς ἀληθῶς ἀγαθὴ θεοῦ ἄγγελος διέτρεχε τὸν κόσμον","chosen":"truly a good messenger of God, ran throughout the world","rationale":"ὡς ἀληθῶς is taken idiomatically as “truly” or “in truth,” affirming the report’s personification rather than introducing a comparison. ἄγγελος retains the ordinary sense “messenger”; “angel of God” is lexically possible but would make the personification more specifically supernatural."},{"source_quote":"προκατορθώσῃ τὴν πολιτείαν","chosen":"first set his way of life right","rationale":"πολιτεία here concerns conduct or manner of life, while προ- supplies the prior action required before enjoying the kingdom."},{"source_quote":"τοῦ δὲ πιστεύεσθαι αὐτὸν χάριν ὅτι θειότητος γέμων ταῦτα ποιεῖ","chosen":"to secure belief in him—that he did these things while full of divinity","rationale":"The ὅτι clause is treated as the content to be believed. It can alternatively be read causally: “to secure belief in him, because he does these things while full of divinity.” “Full of divine power” is a possible contextual rendering of θειότης, but “divinity” preserves the source’s stronger wording."},{"source_quote":"κυλλοὺς ποιεῖ περιπατεῖν, χωλοὺς ἀνορθοῖ","chosen":"he made those with deformed limbs walk and set the lame upright","rationale":"The translation preserves the source’s distinction between κυλλοί, people with bent or deformed limbs, and χωλοί, the lame."},{"source_quote":"λεπροὶ ψωροὶ","chosen":"people with leprosy or scabies","rationale":"The main rendering reads the juxtaposed adjectives as two groups within the surrounding enumeration of afflictions: λεπροί as people with leprosy and ψωροί as people with a scabby or mangy skin condition, here rendered “scabies.” Because there is no conjunction, the words can alternatively describe one group as both leprous and scabby: “scabby leprous people.”"},{"source_quote":"διὰ πλειόνων τῶν ἐπιδημούντων","chosen":"because of the increasing number of visitors","rationale":"ἐπιδημέω describes people visiting or sojourning in a place. “Through more of those arriving” is a more literal alternative."},{"source_quote":"συστήματα κατὰ τόπους","chosen":"Assemblies … in various places","rationale":"συστήματα can denote organized groups, gatherings, or assemblies; the following deliberation and inquiry favor “assemblies.”"}],"source_issues":[{"source_quote":"Ἰουδαίοις τὴν. τοῦ ἀιδίου θεοῦ","issue":"The printed full stop interrupts the article and its dependent genitive, where it cannot function as normal sentence punctuation. The translation provisionally treats it as intrusive editorial punctuation while preserving all the printed words.","proposed_reading":"Ἰουδαίοις τὴν τοῦ ἀιδίου θεοῦ","blocks_publication":false}]},{"reference":"1.7","text":"And indeed, once in that very year, at the autumn equinox, someone stood up publicly and shouted, saying, “Men of Rome, listen! The Son of God is present in Judea, promising eternal life to all who desire it, if they live according to the intention of the Father who sent him. Therefore change your way—from worse things to better, from temporary things to eternal. Recognize that there is one God, the heavenly one, whose world you inhabit unjustly before his righteous eyes. But if you make this change and live according to his will, you will be carried into another age, become everlasting, and enjoy his ineffable good things. If, however, you disobey, your souls will be cast, after the dissolution of the body, into the place of fire, where, while being punished eternally, they will reorient themselves to no avail. For each person’s present life is the time for reorientation.”

As I listened to this, I was distressed that no one from among such great crowds, after hearing so momentous an announcement, had said, “I will travel to Judea so that I may see whether this man is telling the truth when he says these things.” For the claim was that the Son of God had come to Judea and, for the sake of a good and eternal hope, was revealing the will of the Father who sent him—especially since what they say he proclaims is no small matter. For he confidently affirms that the souls of some, being eternal, will enjoy eternal good things, while the souls of others, cast into unquenchable fire, will be punished throughout the age.","lexical_decisions":[{"source_quote":"πρὸς αὐτῷ τῷ ἔτει","chosen":"in that very year","rationale":"The phrase locates the event at or close upon the same year. “About that same year” is also possible."},{"source_quote":"ἐν φθινοπωρινῇ τροπῇ","chosen":"at the autumn equinox","rationale":"As with the corresponding spring expression, τροπή denotes a seasonal turning point; “at the turn of autumn” is a broader alternative."},{"source_quote":"μεταβάλλεσθε τὸν τρόπον","chosen":"change your way","rationale":"The wording calls for a change in manner or way of life. It is kept distinct from the μετανοέω word group later in the passage."},{"source_quote":"ἀνωφέλητα μετανοήσουσιν· ὁ γὰρ τῆς μετανοίας καιρὸς","chosen":"they will reorient themselves to no avail. For … the time for reorientation","rationale":"The cognate verb and noun are rendered consistently under the supplied terminology policy. “They will repent uselessly … the time for repentance” is the traditional alternative; “they will regret it to no avail” would emphasize their futile response under punishment."},{"source_quote":"τῶν ἀπορρήτων αὐτοῦ ἀγαθῶν","chosen":"his ineffable good things","rationale":"ἀπόρρητος can mean secret, forbidden to disclose, or unutterable. “Ineffable” fits the transcendent benefits here while preserving their unspeakable character."},{"source_quote":"ὡς ὅτι υἱὸς θεοῦ ἐπιδεδήμηκεν τῇ Ἰουδαίᾳ","chosen":"For the claim was that the Son of God had come to Judea","rationale":"ὡς ὅτι can introduce reported content or give a causal explanation. The context of a public report favors reported content; “since indeed the Son of God had come” is a viable alternative."},{"source_quote":"υἱὸς θεοῦ","chosen":"the Son of God","rationale":"Although the phrase is anarthrous here, it refers contextually to the previously identified ὁ τοῦ θεοῦ υἱός. “A son of God” remains grammatically possible if the phrase is read indefinitely."},{"source_quote":"τὸν αἰῶνα κολασθήσεσθαι","chosen":"will be punished throughout the age","rationale":"This preserves the explicit noun αἰών rather than automatically assimilating it to the nearby ἀϊδίως. In this context “will be punished forever” is also semantically supportable."}],"source_issues":[{"source_quote":"μετὰ τὴν τοῦ σώματος·λύσιν","issue":"The printed middle dot falls inside the article-genitive-noun phrase and interrupts “the dissolution of the body.” The translation provisionally treats it as intrusive editorial punctuation.","proposed_reading":"μετὰ τὴν τοῦ σώματος λύσιν","blocks_publication":false}]},{"reference":"1.8","text":"While saying these things about others, I also spoke to myself: “Why do I blame others when I am under the same charge of negligence? No, I will set out for Judea after first arranging my own affairs.” Yet although I had resolved on this course, a long period of delay followed, because the affairs of life were difficult to disentangle. At last, then, I somehow reflected on the nature of life: by entangling those who are eager in hope, it lies in wait for them. Moreover, I reflected on how long I had been stealthily drawn in while being tossed about by hopes, and on the fact that we human beings die while occupied in this way. Leaving all my affairs just as they happened to be, I hurried to Portus. After reaching the harbor and putting out to sea, I was carried by the winds’ hostility to Alexandria instead of Judea. Held there for lack of winds, I spent time with the philosophers and recounted the accounts concerning both the report and the man who had appeared in Rome. They replied, “We do not know the man who appeared in Rome. But about the one who had been in Judea and whom the report calls the Son of God, we have also heard from many who have come from there as well, and we have learned about all the wonders he performed by speaking.”","lexical_decisions":[{"source_quote":"πρότερον τὸν ἐμὸν διαθεὶς βίον","chosen":"after first arranging my own affairs","rationale":"διατίθημι with βίος can refer to putting one’s life in order. The following practical delays favor arranging personal affairs rather than an exclusively moral sense."},{"source_quote":"ὡδίποτε τὴν τοῦ βίου φύσιν","chosen":"I somehow reflected on the nature of life","rationale":"ὡδίποτε is taken adverbially as “somehow or other.” The unusual expression is intelligible without conjectural alteration."},{"source_quote":"ἐλπίδι ἐμπλέκων τοὺς σπεύδοντας ἐνεδρεύει","chosen":"by entangling those who are eager in hope, it lies in wait for them","rationale":"Life remains the implied subject of both participle and verb. “Those who hurry” is the more literal alternative for τοὺς σπεύδοντας."},{"source_quote":"ὃν ποτε εἰσεκλάπην χρόνον ἐλπίσιν δονούμενος","chosen":"how long I had been stealthily drawn in while being tossed about by hopes","rationale":"The accusative ὃν … χρόνον is read as duration and represented by “how long,” rather than as the direct object of εἰσεκλάπην. The verb is given its inward and surreptitious force as “had been stealthily drawn in.” “Had slipped in unnoticed” is possible if the passive form is understood intransitively, while “had been inveigled” is another contextual possibility; the destination or situation entered is not expressed."},{"source_quote":"εἰς Πόρτον","chosen":"to Portus","rationale":"The capitalized Πόρτον is treated as Portus, Rome’s harbor settlement, rather than translated generically as “to the port.”"},{"source_quote":"ἀνέμων ἔχθραις","chosen":"by the winds’ hostility","rationale":"This preserves the source’s personifying image rather than reducing the phrase to “because of adverse winds.”"},{"source_quote":"τὰ τῆς φήμης καὶ τοῦ ἐν Ῥώμῃ φανέντος ἔλεγον τοὺς λόγους","chosen":"recounted the accounts concerning both the report and the man who had appeared in Rome","rationale":"The syntax is somewhat redundant, but λόγους can denote accounts concerning both topics. “Related what the report said and the words of the man” is possible, though it more strongly makes the second genitive possessive."},{"source_quote":"τοῦ ἐν Ἰουδαίᾳ γενομένου καὶ υἱοῦ θεοῦ ὑπὸ τῆς φήμης λεγομένου","chosen":"the one who had been in Judea and whom the report calls the Son of God","rationale":"ὑπὸ τῆς φήμης explicitly identifies the report as the source of the designation “Son of God.” The broader γενομένου is rendered neutrally as “had been”; “had appeared” or “had emerged” is possible in context."},{"source_quote":"καὶ παρὰ πολλῶν τῶν κἀκεῖθεν ἐληλυθότων ἠκούσαμεν","chosen":"we have also heard from many who have come from there as well","rationale":"The additive wording is retained without supplying an unexpressed first source for ἠκούσαμεν. That first point of comparison could be Clement’s account or another source; ὑπὸ τῆς φήμης belongs explicitly to the preceding designation, not necessarily to the verb “we have heard.”"},{"source_quote":"ὧν λαλῶν ἐποίει θαυμασίων","chosen":"the wonders he performed by speaking","rationale":"The circumstantial participle λαλῶν can express means, yielding miracles performed by speech. “The wonders he performed while speaking” is also grammatically possible."}],"source_issues":[{"source_quote":"οὐ μὴν ἀλλὰ καὶ. ὃν ποτε","issue":"The printed full stop separates καί from the following relative construction, leaving the latter as an abrupt fragment. The translation provisionally treats the stop as intrusive editorial punctuation while retaining the difficult wording that follows.","proposed_reading":"οὐ μὴν ἀλλὰ καὶ ὃν ποτε","blocks_publication":false},{"source_quote":"ὃν ποτε εἰσεκλάπην χρόνον ἐλπίσιν δονούμενος","issue":"The supplied wording is lexically and syntactically unusual but is retained unchanged. The translation provisionally reads χρόνον as an accusative of duration and εἰσεκλάπην with passive inward or surreptitious-entry force. An intransitive understanding such as “had slipped in unnoticed” remains possible; no conjectural correction is proposed.","proposed_reading":"","blocks_publication":false}]}]}
